  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1243 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nBy Wednesday the 500 MB trough evolving over the central U.S. \nwill have two distinct cores, the northern one sinking across \nIowa as discussed above, and the southern one swinging into \nMississippi. These two cores will then interact with each other\nthrough the latter part of this week, however the nature of\ntheir evolution is still uncertain with a variety of model\nsolutions available. The ECMWF quickly consolidates them into\none closed low over the lower Ohio River valley, with the higher\nmoisture content of the southern/Gulf system wrapping clear\naround and back-dooring into Illinois and even eastern Iowa. The\nGFS, meanwhile, catapults the southern core around the northern\ncore and then quickly up the Atlantic seaboard as a nor'easter,\nwhile stranding the remaining core behind to spin somewhere near\nthe MO/IL/KY region. That leaves less moisture content available\nfor entrainment around the northern low, however, nearly all\nsolutions to depict a large cloud and precipitation shield\ndeveloping around that northern system, with its northwestern\nfringes backing across Illinois and into at least far\nsoutheastern Iowa around Thanksgiving day. The primary\nuncertainties are thus how far westward the edge of the\nprecipitation shield will extend (i.e., whether it will reach\nour forecast area or not), and how much precipitation will fall.\nCertainly any relatively heavier precip amounts will remain to\nour southeast, closer to the core of the low over Illinois and\nIndiana, and possibly Kentucky and eastern Missouri, with little\nto no precipitation accumulation currently anticipated in our\nsoutheastern counties. However, some light snow or possibly\nrain/snow mix could occur in that area on Thanksgiving, and we\nwill keep an eye on this potential in the coming days.\n\nThe storm system should finally eject eastward up the Ohio River\nvalley and into the eastern U.S. around Thursday night and\nFriday. In its wake a fairly brisk, broadly west northwesterly\nflow regime will set in over the Midwest and Iowa through this\nweekend. A series of troughs will pass quickly through this flow\nand provide opportunities for additional precipitation in our\narea, however, attempting to discern any details of timing and\nstrength of these systems is not feasible at this range. In such\nscenarios where the troughs in question will not even move over\nthe North American continent and its observational network for\nseveral days yet, the best that can be said is that there will\nbe intermittent precipitation chances, and also steadily\nmoderating temperatures that will allow questions as to whether\nany such precipitation would fall as snow or rain. These\nconsiderations will be monitored throughout the week.",
